\title{Memory Editing as Algebraic Quotienting}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 19, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop an algebraic model of compositional memory in which finite experience streams form a free monoid and a memory mechanism is a monoid homomorphism into a representation monoid. This framework separates collisions between distinct streams, complete erasure to the neutral representation, and the congruence of observational indistinguishability. We prove that every finite-state memory over a nonempty alphabet is lossy, that completely erased streams form a submonoid, and that the observable memory algebra is isomorphic to the quotient of the stream monoid by indistinguishability. We then study targeted forgetting, a symbolwise policy that deletes rejected experiences while retaining the order of accepted ones. Every compositional map respecting the policy's identifications factors uniquely through its quotient. The results provide a unified account of bounded memory, redaction, event filtering, and downstream compatibility.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

A memory mechanism receives a sequence of experiences and produces a representation. The representation may be the state of a finite controller, a rolling database summary, a filtered event log, or a compressed feature. In each case sequential composition is fundamental: processing one stream after another should correspond to combining their representations.

We express that principle by taking histories to be elements of a free monoid and memories to be monoid homomorphisms. A memory map then determines an equivalence relation on streams: two streams are equivalent exactly when they produce the same representation. Because the map respects composition, this equivalence is a congruence. Collapsing every class to one point yields a quotient monoid isomorphic to the reachable representation algebra. Thus observable memory is precisely the stream algebra after all invisible distinctions have been removed.

Three consequences organize the theory. A nonempty alphabet generates infinitely many streams, so a finite representation cannot distinguish them all. Streams sent to the neutral representation form a submonoid. Finally, symbolwise deletion induces a quotient with a universal property: every compatible downstream homomorphism operates uniquely on edited memories.

\section{Algebraic framework}

\begin{definition}[Monoid and experience stream]
A \emph{monoid} is a set $M$ with an associative product and an identity $1_M$. For an alphabet $\alpha$, an \emph{experience stream} is a finite word over $\alpha$. All such streams form the free monoid $\Astar$ under concatenation, with the empty stream $\eps$ as identity.
\end{definition}

The universal property of $\Astar$ says that any assignment of symbols to a monoid extends uniquely to a monoid homomorphism from streams. Once representations of elementary experiences are fixed, representations of all finite streams are therefore determined compositionally.

\begin{definition}[Compositional memory]
Let $R$ be a monoid. A \emph{compositional memory map} is a function $m:\Astar\to R$ such that
\[
m(\eps)=1_R,\qquad m(xy)=m(x)m(y)
\]
for all $x,y\in\Astar$.
\end{definition}

The observable range is the submonoid
\[
\im(m)=\{m(x):x\in\Astar\}.
\]
The distinction between $R$ and $\im(m)$ matters: some nominal representation states may be unreachable.

\begin{definition}[Erasure and indistinguishability]
The erased-stream set is
\[
E_m=\{x\in\Astar:m(x)=1_R\}.
\]
Observational indistinguishability is the relation
\[
x\sim_m y\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad m(x)=m(y).
\]
\end{definition}

This relation is an equivalence. It is also multiplicatively compatible: if $x\sim_m y$ and $u\sim_m v$, then
\[
m(xu)=m(x)m(u)=m(y)m(v)=m(yv),
\]
so $xu\sim_m yv$. Hence $\sim_m$ is a monoid congruence. The set $E_m$ is exactly the class $[\eps]_{\sim_m}$; complete erasure is one distinguished part of the larger pattern of information loss.

For any congruence $\sim$ on a monoid $M$, the quotient $M/{\sim}$ consists of classes $[x]$ with multiplication $[x][y]=[xy]$. Compatibility makes this independent of representatives. The canonical quotient map is $q(x)=[x]$.

\section{Finite memory and unavoidable collisions}

\begin{theorem}[Finite-Memory Loss]
Let $\alpha$ be nonempty, let $R$ be a finite monoid, and let $m:\Astar\to R$ be a compositional memory map. Then there are distinct streams $x,y\in\Astar$ for which $m(x)=m(y)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Choose $a\in\alpha$. The words $\eps,a,a^2,a^3,\ldots$ are pairwise distinct, so $\Astar$ is infinite. No function from an infinite set to a finite set is injective. The pigeonhole principle therefore supplies distinct $x$ and $y$ with equal memories.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Nontrivial Indistinguishability]
Under the same assumptions, at least one $\sim_m$-class contains two distinct streams.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The collision in the preceding theorem is precisely a pair $x\ne y$ satisfying $x\sim_m y$.
\end{proof}

The collision need not occur at the neutral state. A finite memory can have very few completely erased streams while still identifying many nonempty histories.

For a finite alphabet of size $k$, the number of words of length at most $n$ is
\[
N(k,n)=\sum_{j=0}^{n}k^j=
\begin{cases}
(k^{n+1}-1)/(k-1),&k>1,\\
n+1,&k=1.
\end{cases}
\]
If a memory has $s$ states and $N(k,n)>s$, two words of length at most $n$ must collide. This finite-horizon count sharpens where a collision must appear, while compositionality explains how all such collisions fit into a congruence.

\section{The algebra of complete erasure}

\begin{theorem}[Erasure Closure]
For every compositional memory $m:\Astar\to R$, the set $E_m$ is a submonoid. Explicitly, $\eps\in E_m$, and if $x,y\in E_m$, then $xy\in E_m$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Identity preservation gives $m(\eps)=1_R$. If $m(x)=m(y)=1_R$, then
\[
m(xy)=m(x)m(y)=1_R1_R=1_R.
\]
Thus the identity belongs to $E_m$ and $E_m$ is closed under concatenation.
\end{proof}

Moreover, inserting an erased stream into any context is invisible: for $e\in E_m$,
\[
m(xey)=m(x)m(e)m(y)=m(x)m(y)=m(xy).
\]
The converse direction is not generally available: $m(xy)=1_R$ need not imply that either factor is neutral. Therefore the erased set is a submonoid but need not be factor-closed.

\section{Observable memory as a quotient}

\begin{theorem}[Observable Quotient Theorem]
For every compositional memory map $m:\Astar\to R$, there is a monoid isomorphism
\[
\Astar/{\sim_m}\cong\im(m).
\]
It sends $[x]$ to $m(x)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Define $\Phi([x])=m(x)$. If $[x]=[y]$, then $x\sim_m y$, so $m(x)=m(y)$ and the definition is independent of representatives. Also,
\[
\Phi([x][y])=m(xy)=m(x)m(y)=\Phi([x])\Phi([y]).
\]
Surjectivity follows from the definition of $\im(m)$. If $\Phi([x])=\Phi([y])$, then $m(x)=m(y)$, so $[x]=[y]$; hence $\Phi$ is injective.
\end{proof}

The theorem states that a representation remembers exactly one equivalence class of histories. It also explains why the image, rather than the entire codomain, is the appropriate observable algebra.

\begin{theorem}[Finite Memory: Loss, Erasure, and Quotient]
Let $\alpha$ be nonempty, let $R$ be finite, and let $m:\Astar\to R$ be compositional. Then:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item distinct streams collide under $m$;
\item $E_m$ contains $\eps$ and is closed under concatenation; and
\item $\Astar/{\sim_m}$ is isomorphic to $\im(m)$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Combine the Finite-Memory Loss, Erasure Closure, and Observable Quotient theorems.
\end{proof}

\section{Targeted forgetting}

Let a retention policy be a function $r:\alpha\to\{0,1\}$, where $1$ means retain and $0$ means erase.

\begin{definition}[Targeted-forgetting map]
The map $T_r:\Astar\to\Astar$ is defined on symbols by
\[
T_r(a)=
\begin{cases}
a,&r(a)=1,\\
\eps,&r(a)=0,
\end{cases}
\]
and extended by concatenation. Thus $T_r$ deletes rejected symbols while preserving the order and multiplicity of retained symbols.
\end{definition}

For $w=a_1\cdots a_n$,
\[
T_r(w)=T_r(a_1)\cdots T_r(a_n).
\]
Consequently $T_r(uv)=T_r(u)T_r(v)$ and $T_r(\eps)=\eps$.

\begin{proposition}[Rejected Symbols Are Erased]
If $r(a)=0$, then $T_r(a)=\eps$, so the one-letter stream $a$ belongs to the erased-stream submonoid of $T_r$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
This is the rejected branch in the definition. Erasure closure then shows that every word made solely from rejected symbols is erased.
\end{proof}

Two streams are indistinguishable under $T_r$ exactly when deleting rejected symbols gives the same retained subsequence. The image consists exactly of words containing only retained symbols: deletion cannot output a rejected symbol, while every retained-only word is fixed by $T_r$.

\begin{corollary}[Targeted Forgetting as a Quotient]
For every policy $r$,
\[
\Astar/{\sim_{T_r}}\cong\im(T_r).
\]
The right-hand side is the embedded free monoid of retained-only words.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply the Observable Quotient Theorem to $T_r$ and use the preceding description of its image.
\end{proof}

\section{Universal property}

\begin{theorem}[Universal Targeted-Forgetting Theorem]
Let $S$ be a monoid and $g:\Astar\to S$ a monoid homomorphism. Assume that
\[
T_r(x)=T_r(y)\quad\Longrightarrow\quad g(x)=g(y)
\]
for every $x,y\in\Astar$. Then there is a unique monoid homomorphism
\[
\bar g:\Astar/{\sim_{T_r}}\to S
\]
such that $g=\bar g\circ q$, where $q(x)=[x]$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Set $\bar g([x])=g(x)$. The assumption makes this well-defined: equivalent representatives have equal $g$-values. Multiplicativity follows from
\[
\bar g([x][y])=g(xy)=g(x)g(y)=\bar g([x])\bar g([y]).
\]
The equality $g=\bar g\circ q$ holds by construction. If $h$ is another such factor, then every quotient element is $[x]$ for some $x$, and
\[
h([x])=h(q(x))=g(x)=\bar g([x]),
\]
so $h=\bar g$.
\end{proof}

A downstream compositional computation can therefore be performed after editing exactly when it is constant on the policy's classes. For example, the number of retained symbols depends only on $T_r(w)$ and factors through the quotient. By contrast, the number of rejected symbols does not: words with identical edited outputs can contain different numbers of deleted symbols.

\section{Algorithms and numerical experiments}

\subsection{Targeted deletion}
Scan a word from left to right and append a symbol precisely when the policy retains it. After processing the first $i$ symbols, the output equals the targeted-forgetting image of the first $i$-symbol prefix. This invariant proves correctness by induction. For a word of length $n$, the algorithm uses $O(n)$ policy queries, $O(n)$ time under constant-time symbol handling, and at most $O(n)$ output space.

\subsection{Collision search}
For an explicit finite-state memory, enumerate words and store the first representative seen for each state. A repeated state yields two distinct streams with the same memory. Processing $N$ words costs $O(N)$ memory evaluations and stores at most $\min(N,s)$ representatives for $s$ states. More than $s$ tested words guarantee a collision.

\subsection{Finite quotient enumeration}
To inspect classes among words of length at most $n$, group words by memory value. With hashable states, grouping $N(k,n)$ words takes expected $O(N(k,n))$ dictionary operations in addition to memory evaluation. For targeted forgetting on $\{a,b,c\}$ retaining only $a$ and $c$, the words $ac$, $abc$, $abbc$, and $bacb$ all map to $ac$.

\section{Applications}

\paragraph{Privacy-preserving processing.}
A symbolwise redaction policy removes sensitive event types. The quotient consists of histories visible after redaction. The universal property gives an exact compatibility criterion: a deterministic compositional analysis can run after redaction precisely when raw histories with equal redacted outputs always receive equal analytical results. This criterion describes functional dependence; it does not by itself establish statistical privacy or resistance to side channels.

\paragraph{Finite-state systems.}
Input words can be represented in a finite transformation monoid. Collisions are unavoidable, and the induced congruence records equal observable actions. This creates a bridge to automata and formal-language theory.

\paragraph{Event sourcing.}
A bounded current state cannot uniquely encode every arbitrarily long event history. The quotient identifies histories compatible with one summary, while $E_m$ records batches with neutral aggregate effect. Domain semantics remain necessary before neutral batches are discarded from audit records.

\paragraph{Sequence features.}
A compositional feature extractor induces representational invariances through its congruence. With finite precision, collisions reflect capacity limits; with deliberately structured maps, they may represent intended invariances.

\section{Limitations and future work}

The model uses exact equality and deterministic composition. Probabilistic or approximate memory calls for distributions, metrics, divergences, or Markov kernels. The homomorphism law is also strong, although state-update systems can often be represented through transformation monoids.

Quantitative work should bound the largest collision class among words of length at most $n$. Finite-index congruences should be related to Myhill--Nerode theory, syntactic monoids, and minimal automata. For targeted deletion, a full normalization theory can identify the quotient explicitly with the free monoid on the retained subtype.

Successive policies should correspond to intersection of retained alphabets, producing a lattice of policies and contravariant quotient maps. Richer representation structures lead to normal-subgroup, ideal, or submodule quotients. Probabilistic variants may support entropy-loss and data-processing results. Finally, categorical treatment through coequalizers or regular epimorphisms should make forgetting functorial and clarify products and iterated quotients.

\section{Conclusion}

Compositional memory maps streams into an algebra of representations. Finite capacity forces distinct histories to collide. Completely erased streams form a submonoid, all collisions form a congruence, and the quotient by that congruence is exactly the reachable memory algebra. For targeted forgetting, rejected symbols disappear, retained outputs form the observable range, and every compatible downstream map factors uniquely through the quotient. Forgetting is therefore not merely missing storage: it is a structured transformation of which histories count as observably the same.

\end{document}

